Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement. Question: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts? Option A: No; Option B: Yes.

Merger Agreement: "Material Adverse Effect" means any occurrence, event, change or effect that (i) materially adversely affects the business, operations, or financial condition of the Company, or (ii) prevents, delays, or impairs the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions contemplated herein. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following carveouts shall apply solely to the assessment of changes in business operations and financial performance: (a) adverse effects arising from global economic downturns; (b) regulatory or legislative changes of general applicability; (c) routine fluctuations in financial market conditions including minor variations in interest rates or stock prices; and (d) effects stemming solely from the public announcement or pendency of this Agreement. It is expressly provided, however, that any impairment to the Company's ability to consummate the transactions shall not benefit from these carveouts and shall be fully considered in the determination of a Material Adverse Effect. (See Article 7.2 and Section 3.3 for further details.)
Choices:

