Instruction: Read the following excerpt from a Merger Agreement and then answer the question by selecting the best option. Question: Is the 'ability to consummate' provision in this Merger Agreement subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?

Merger Agreement Excerpt: "For purposes of this Agreement, a 'Material Adverse Effect' shall mean any change, event, or circumstance that, either individually or in aggregate with other events, would materially impede, delay, or impair the Company’s ability to consummate the Merger. Notwithstanding the foregoing, any failure, delay, or impairment solely resulting from: (i) general economic downturns; (ii) uniform fluctuations in commodity prices affecting the entire industry; (iii) industry-wide regulatory revisions; or (iv) shifts in capital market conditions, shall not be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Effect. In addition, any adverse impact arising from external market conditions or pervasive political instability that is common to all industry participants is explicitly excluded, unless such impact is uniquely severe and disproportionate to the effects observed among the Company’s peers."
